=== EXAMPLE 1: PowerShell Empire (HTTP C2) ===
Rule (ET SID 2027512):
  alert http $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (
    msg:"ET MALWARE Possible PowerShell Empire Activity Outbound";
    flow:established,to_server;
    content:"GET"; http_method;
    content:"/"; http_uri; depth:1;
    pcre:"/^(?:login\/process|admin\/get|news)\.php$/RU";
    content:"session="; http_cookie;
    pcre:"/^(?:[A-Z0-9+/]{4})*(?:[A-Z0-9+/]{2}==|[A-Z0-9+/]{3}=|[A-Z0-9+/]{4})$/CRi";
    content:"Mozilla|2f|5.0|20 28|Windows|20|NT|20|6.1"; http_user_agent; http_start;
    content:".php|20|HTTP|2f|1.1|0d 0a|Cookie|3a 20|session="; fast_pattern; http_header_names;
    content:!"Referer"; content:!"Cache"; content:!"Accept";
    sid:2027512; rev:1;
  )

Why it works:
  → URI must be login/process.php, admin/get.php, or news.php (Empire agent URIs)
  → Cookie 'session=' value must be base64-encoded string
  → User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 6.1 (Empire default)
  → Missing Referer, Cache, Accept headers (non-browser pattern)
  → PCRE CRi: cookie buffer (C), relative (R), case-insensitive (i)

PCAP for testing: /home/htb-student/pcaps/psempire.pcap
Agency reference: https://github.com/EmpireProject/Empire/blob/master/data/agent/agent.ps1#L78

=== EXAMPLE 2: Covenant C2 (Body Content) ===
Rule (SID 3000011):
  alert tcp any any -> $HOME_NET any (
    msg:"detected by body";
    content:"<title>Hello World!</title>";
    detection_filter: track by_src, count 4, seconds 10;
    priority:1;
    sid:3000011;
  )

Why it works:
  → Covenant C2 HTTP profile returns <title>Hello World!</title>
  → detection_filter: must fire ≥4 times in 10s from same src (reduces FP)
  → Reference: https://github.com/cobbr/Covenant/blob/master/Covenant/Data/Profiles/DefaultHttpProfile.yaml#L35

Note: rule is intentionally marked 'inefficient' — false positives possible
PCAP for testing: /home/htb-student/pcaps/covenant.pcap

=== EXAMPLE 3: Covenant C2 (Beacon Size / Analytics) ===
Rule (SID 3000001):
  alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> any any (
    msg:"detected by size and counter";
    dsize:312;
    detection_filter: track by_src, count 3, seconds 10;
    priority:1;
    sid:3000001;
  )

Why it works:
  → Covenant heartbeat = fixed 312-byte payload
  → detection_filter: ≥3 occurrences from same src in 10s
  → Anomaly-based detection — no content inspection needed
  → Harder to evade than string patterns

=== EXAMPLE 4: Sliver C2 (HTTP URI) ===
Rule (SID 1000007):
  alert tcp any any -> any any (
    msg:"Sliver C2 Implant Detected";
    content:"POST";
    pcre:"/\/(php|api|upload|actions|rest|v1|oauth2callback|authenticate|oauth2|oauth|auth|database|db|namespaces)(.*?)((login|signin|api|samples|rpc|index|admin|register|sign-up)\.php)\?[a-z_]{1,2}=[a-z0-9]{1,10}/i";
    sid:1000007; rev:1;
  )

Sliver Set-Cookie rule (SID 1000003):
  alert tcp any any -> any any (
    msg:"Sliver C2 Implant Detected - Cookie";
    content:"Set-Cookie";
    pcre:"/(PHPSESSID|SID|SSID|APISID|csrf-state|AWSALBCORS)\=[a-z0-9]{32}\;/";
    sid:1000003; rev:1;
  )

Why it works:
  → Sliver HTTP profile uses specific directory/file name patterns for URIs
  → Set-Cookie name is fixed (PHPSESSID/SID/etc.) with 32-char hex value
  → Reference: https://github.com/BishopFox/sliver/blob/master/server/configs/http-c2.go#L294

PCAP for testing: /home/htb-student/pcaps/sliver.pcap

=== EXAMPLE 5: Dridex (TLS Certificate Inspection) ===
Rule (ET SID 2023476):
  alert tls $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET any (
    msg:"ET MALWARE ABUSE.CH SSL Blacklist Malicious SSL certificate detected (Dridex)";
    flow:established,from_server;
    content:"|16|"; content:"|0b|"; within:8;     ← TLS handshake + certificate type
    byte_test:3,<,1200,0,relative;
    content:"|03 02 01 02 02 09 00|"; fast_pattern;
    content:"|30 09 06 03 55 04 06 13 02|"; distance:0;  ← OID countryName
    pcre:"/^[A-Z]{2}/R";               ← country code format
    content:"|55 04 07|"; distance:0;   ← OID localityName
    content:"|55 04 0a|"; distance:0;   ← OID organizationName
    content:"|55 04 03|"; distance:0;   ← OID commonName
    byte_test:1,>,13,1,relative;        ← CN length > 13
    content:!"www."; distance:2; within:4;
    pcre:"...";                         ← DGA-like CN pattern
    content:!"|2a 86 48 86 f7 0d 01 09 01|";  ← not email OID
    content:!"GoDaddy";
    sid:2023476; rev:5;
  )

Key OIDs:
  |30 09 06 03 55 04 06 13 02| → countryName (2.5.4.6)
  |55 04 07|                   → localityName (2.5.4.7)
  |55 04 0a|                   → organizationName (2.5.4.10)
  |55 04 03|                   → commonName (2.5.4.3)

PCAP for testing: /home/htb-student/pcaps/dridex.pcap
Reference: https://unit42.paloaltonetworks.com/wireshark-tutorial-dridex-infection-traffic/

=== EXAMPLE 6: Sliver (TLS JA3 Fingerprint) ===
Rule (SID 1002):
  alert tls any any -> any any (
    msg:"Sliver C2 SSL";
    ja3.hash;
    content:"473cd7cb9faa642487833865d516e578";
    sid:1002; rev:1;
  )

How to get JA3 hash:
  ja3 -a --json /home/htb-student/pcaps/sliverenc.pcap
    → Shows ja3_digest: 473cd7cb9faa642487833865d516e578
    → JA3 derived from Client Hello: TLS version + cipher suites + extensions + elliptic curves
    → Different Sliver versions = different JA3 hashes

PCAP for testing: /home/htb-student/pcaps/sliverenc.pcap
JA3 tool: https://github.com/salesforce/ja3

=== APPROACH COMPARISON ===
Signature:  content matches  → precise, known threats, bypassable with obfuscation
Analytics:  dsize + counter  → behavioral, obfuscation-resistant, higher FP rate
TLS certs:  OID byte match   → works on encrypted traffic, cert must be default
JA3 hash:   TLS fingerprint  → very precise, breaks on TLS version/cipher changes
